-- title:
--   Box-motion velocities vanish outside the middle of the period
-- statement:
--   A box motion from layout $A$ to layout $B$ moves box $i$ smoothly, with centre $c_i(s)$ and half-widths $w_i(s)$, from its position in $A$ (for $s\le0$) to its position in $B$ (for $s\ge1$). Volumes are constant along the way, the boxes stay $\eta$-separated, and boxes with safe indices stay in $\{x_0>2\}$. Its velocity at time $t$ is the sum of the moving-box velocities of the boxes at time $s=2t-\frac12$, so the motion happens during $\frac14\le t\le\frac34$. Then the velocity $u$ of a box motion has time collars:
--   $$
--   u(t,\cdot)=0\qquad\text{for }t<\tfrac14\text{ and for }t>\tfrac34 .
--   $$
--
--   So box motions can be repeated periodically and preceded by the loading phase without overlapping.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/MathematicalPhysics/NavierStokes/BalancedTransport/MotionField.lean#L181-L202

